	<body>
		<outline text="Links To My Other Sites" created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:42:23 GMT" collapse="true" flBulletedSubs="true">
			<outline text="My Now Page" created="Sat, 28 Feb 2026 16:02:57 GMT" type="link" url="https://info.frankmcpherson.net/whatIsFrankDoing.opml"/>
			<outline text="My Blog" created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:42:33 GMT" type="link" url="https://frankmcpherson.blog"/>
			<outline text="My &quot;Old School&quot; Drummer blog" created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:42:42 GMT" type="link" url="https://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/"/>
			<outline text="My Tech outline" created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:43:07 GMT" type="link" url="http://tech.frankm.info"/>
			</outline>
		<outline text="October 2026" created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:00:40 GMT" name="october2026" type="calendarMonth">
			<outline text="October 27" created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:47:20 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="27" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I wrote &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/2026/10/26/boosting-xfinity-internet-speed.html&quot;&gt;a blog post&lt;/a&gt; detailing my experience troubleshooting Xfinity's 1 Gbps Internet service." created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:47:20 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 25" created="Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:25:34 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="25" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="We resolved the Xfinity Internet speed issue I was experiencing by enabling NAT boost on the TP-Link router/access point we use. I am going to write a blog post to summarize the issue and resolution, which would not have been resolved if not for Claude. " created="Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:25:34 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Key steps to resolution were testing using a Raspberry Pi 4 directly connected to the cable modem and using the Ookla terminal speedtest app so that web browser CPU overhead wasn't a factor. I had also previously installed speedtest-cli that is a Python based app not provided by Ookla and not as good. The use of the terminal app enabled me to confirm the 1 Gbps service is enabled on the line and therefore pointed to issue at the router. Given the router name and version, Claude recommended enabling NAT boost after which I then saw the 1 Gbps speeds. I am now seeing the 1 Gbps speeds consistently via the Speedtest container I am running on my network." created="Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:27:23 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 24" created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:40:19 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="24" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Bought New Cable Modem" created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:53:23 GMT" collapse="true">
					<outline text="On Thursday I received an email from Xfinity saying that my cable modem was due to be upgraded. I could either rent the Xfinity Wifi Gateway and or buy my own cable modem like I had been using. Xfinity provided a list of certified cable modems and I selected and bought the Hitron Coda56. It looks to me that the two key differences are the support for DOCSIS 3.1 (previous model only supported up to DOCSIS 3.0) and a 2.5 Gbps LAN port, prior one was 1 Gbps." created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:40:19 GMT"/>
					<outline text="A while back I upgraded by Xfinity cable and Internet package for a lower rate than I was paying previously but it came with 1 Gbps service. After receiving the &quot;upgrade&quot; my Internet speed testing averaged 300 Mbps, which is what I was seeing previously and because I didn't necessarily need faster service and I was actually paying less than what I was for 400 Mbps service, I lived with the performance." created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:45:24 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I thought that maybe buying the new cable modem would improve the performance closer to the 1 Gbps I should be seeing, but to my surprise that has not been the case. My troubleshooting includes connecting a computer directly to the cable modem using Cat 6 cable and with that I saw 485 Mbps, better but still not where it should be. " created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:49:08 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="Seeking Help from Xfinity" created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:52:13 GMT" collapse="true">
					<outline text="Installation of the new Cable modem requires using the Xfinity app on my phone to activate it once it is connected to the coaxial cable. The activation process never indicated complete in the app although after the first run our Internet access worked, but as far as the app was concerned the modem was offline. I ran the activation a couple more times but the app status never changed. I then went to xfinity.com and accessed my account and saw there two cable modems, one, the old one, offline, and another, I assume the new one, online." created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:53:00 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The activation process involves an AI bot, you don't really select an options in the app, is all &quot;tell the bot what you want to do.&quot; I then told the bot, my Internet is working but I am not seeing the speed I should be seeing. It attempted to check signals and equipment health, said everything was fine that is where the &quot;troubleshooting ended.&quot; When I asked to speak with a human I could find no option. When you call Xfinity's phone number there is no option to speak with an operator, it just sends a link to the same AI bot. So, I gave up." created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:03:37 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Shortly later I got a text asking me to provide feedback on my service with Xfinity. Of course, I said I would not recommend them and that my problem is not solved. This morning, while I was doing some more of my own testing, I got a phone call from Xfinity asking about my feedback. Of course, the offshore person listened politely, said they were doing testing on their end, which I assume is the same as the bot did previously, in fact I suspect she just used the same bot to do what I did. With no resolution to my problem she promised to escalate my issue and the call ended. I have my doubts about whether they will call. " created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:07:55 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="Next steps" created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:18:07 GMT">
					<outline text="The only computer I have with physical Ethernet port that I can use for testing is a Raspberry Pi 4, for which the ethernet port is rated for 1 Gbps. I need to run iperf3 testing to confirm the port speed using servers on my home network. I basically have to rule out all possible bottlenecks on the home network to correctly place blame on Xfinity's cable." created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:18:11 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I also have Jarvis searching the Internet for whether other Xfinity customers have similar problems and whether they resolve it and how. I am also seeking an explanation for how 1 Gbps service is possible on the coax cable running to my house. I think there are physical limitations of that cable so that faster service may require compression requiring Xfinity's own equipment. " created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:21:07 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 19" created="Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:36:37 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="19" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="We returned from our annual fall trip to the U.P. on Sunday." created="Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:36:37 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 9" created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:12:15 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="09" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://rsschat.johnj.info/&quot;&gt;This instance of RSS Chat&lt;/a&gt; is said to be running on a Raspberry Pi. The whole stream could be viewed as a blog. " created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:36:51 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.kynovaxtechnologies.com/apps/localmind&quot;&gt;LocalMind&lt;/a&gt; is an AI assistant that runs on Android. Claims everything stays on the phone. As we see more of these type of things, how do we trust the developers?" created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:18:41 GMT"/>
				<outline text="What is &lt;a href=&quot;https://support.claude.com/en/articles/16923645-get-started-with-claude-docs&quot;&gt;Claude Docs&lt;/a&gt; and what can it be used for?" created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:12:15 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 8" created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:46:36 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I found out yesterday that the instance of &lt;a href=&quot;https://archivebox.io/&quot;&gt;ArchiveBox&lt;/a&gt; that I have in my home lab was not accessible. ArchiveBox is deployed in a Docker container and I found a recent release made significant changes to the expected file structure. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:46:36 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I had to do two things. First, I had to change the host to container port mapping so that requests went to the port the app was listening to. The first time I ran that I saw a &quot;new installation&quot; setup screen, which I configured for private network access via a remote proxy. After I got in the app I found that while the snapshot index was available, none of the snapshots were accessed. Further reading of &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/ArchiveBox/ArchiveBox/wiki/Upgrading&quot;&gt;the documentation&lt;/a&gt; pointed me to running a update with a --migrate-only flag to move files to a different directory. Previously all snapshot directories were subdirectories of /archive but moved to /archive/personas/Default. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:48:57 GMT"/>
				<outline text="The problem occurred because I run &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/containrrr/watchtower&quot;&gt;WatchTower&lt;/a&gt; on the host that automatically updates the containers and I did not know about the changes. I had to spend several hours to figure it out. Noticed that WatchTower is no longer being maintained, so I decided to stop that container on the hosts running it." created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:52:16 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 5" created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:00:41 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="05" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Had a outage here due to an issue with Caddy. Rebooted the server and we are back up and running. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:00:40 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="September 2026" created="Tue, 01 Sep 2026 16:19:45 GMT" name="september2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="September 28" created="Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:00:16 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="28" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="A while back an outage to the Drummer &quot;old school&quot; blog publishing function lead to &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/drummerCms/issues/4#issuecomment-3873457630&quot;&gt;my research&lt;/a&gt; about how to enable it for HTTPS. We got it to work but I ultimately decided to stop relying on that functionality and hosting and instead reverted to using pagePark to simply render this continual outline for my work notes. " created="Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:00:16 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I've come to like this format better because I made a way to render this entire outline to a markdown file that can then be used as a source for Google's NotebookLM. I also rendered all of the source OPML files for prior years to markdown and upload those files to a notebook, with the result being that I have a way to chat/search through my prior daynotes." created="Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:02:54 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://andysylvester.com/2026/09/27/creating-a-local-version-of-a-drummer-oldschool-blog/&quot;&gt;Andy Sylvester used my research to build a way to generate a Drummer blog to a local file system&lt;/a&gt; with the result being that it can be hosted on a platform other than the one owned and maintained by Dave Winer. If I understand the process and given there are other tools available other than Drummer for editing OPML it means one can continue using the formatting/templating of &quot;old school&quot; that Dave created in manner that is not dependent on Dave. " created="Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:04:57 GMT"/>
				<outline text="NOTE: I am still dependent on Dave because I am using Drummer to write and edit this content and using pagePark, albeit on my own server, to server it. Drummer is hosted on a server that Dave owns and thus I continue to be at risk of that server going offline and I therefore needing to scramble for an alternate method to edit this file. I do have a copy of Electric Drummer on my Mac but it relies on Dave's authentication method to use the app. What I wish for is a selfhosted version of Drummer." created="Mon, 28 Sep 2026 16:08:34 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 23" created="Wed, 23 Sep 2026 18:50:45 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="23" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Installed &lt;a href=&quot;https://neko.m1k1o.net/&quot;&gt;neko container&lt;/a&gt; on docker.home. It is a container that hosts either Firefox or Chromium and can useful as a throw-away and sandboxed browser access. It's pretty easy to set up with the provided docker docker compose file, I just had to provide the host IP address and change the port. Performance is pretty laggy but seems to render sites fine and audio passes through. Tested streaming YouTube. " created="Wed, 23 Sep 2026 18:50:45 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 21" created="Mon, 21 Sep 2026 18:04:45 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="21" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Found out over the weekend that the new Siri AI has been downloaded and installed on my Macbook. I now have the Siri app on the dock. Time to determine how to use it." created="Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:59:04 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I am seeing more news about Googlebooks and I don't understand why anyone would buy one of these things. Looks like Google wants them to have premium hardware and the UI looks no different than any other Linux distribution. I guess in a way this is an attempt to sell Linux notebooks commercially,  but there nothing here that is different." created="Mon, 21 Sep 2026 18:04:45 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 19" created="Sat, 19 Sep 2026 16:59:32 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="19" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Learned about a new free digital version of a cosmic picture from &lt;a href=&quot;https://cosmicbackground.io&quot;&gt;Cosmicbackground.io&lt;/a&gt; that I downloaded to use as the lock screen wallpaper on my Pixel 10. I also decided to use it as the wallpaper on my Macbook." created="Sat, 19 Sep 2026 16:59:32 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 18" created="Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:55:15 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="18" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="All of the reviews that I have seen of macOS 27 focus on the Siri AI features that are currently not available on my Macbook because they weren't installed during the upgrade. It's labeled in settings as beta and I joined the waitlist but have no eta for availability. Apparently the reason is that this feature is an additional 10 GB download, so they need to control the bandwidth of their servers hosting the download files. Because Siri AI is so prominent, I think the upgrade itself should be delayed until everything can be downloaded." created="Fri, 18 Sep 2026 20:22:26 GMT"/>
				<outline text="First full day running on macOS 27, let's see what I can find. " created="Fri, 18 Sep 2026 15:55:15 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 17" created="Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:16:50 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="17" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I've upgraded the Macbook Pro to macOS 27, which means I am now having my first experience with Liquid Glass. The jury is still out on Glass, but my first question is, why is it that I cannot make the System Settings window wider? I ran in to this when changing the wallpaper, noticing more options that appear to be to the left of the window with no way to access with a mouse. It looks like an incredibly poor oversight by Apple. Found out later this has been a thing with the Settings windows since well before this release of the operating system. I did find a workaround, however." created="Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:24:35 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I installed MX Linux on my first Macbook Air bought way back in 2010 that only has a Intel Core 2 Duo processor and 4 GB of RAM. The OS installed just fine but of course the challenge is using it within the RAM and processor constraints. One thing I do notice is that it has a fan and the fan kicks in more frequently than with my other computers." created="Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:53:35 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I am having trouble with the Zen browser loading the current page of a specific site. It seems no matter how I refresh it always loads that site from cache rather than updating from the server, and that is frustrating. I've deleted the site's data from the browser cache and reloaded, we will see how long that sticks." created="Thu, 17 Sep 2026 16:16:50 GMT" flBulletedSubs="true"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 16" created="Wed, 16 Sep 2026 17:33:45 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="16" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Android 17 QPR1 update is currently installing on my Pixel 10." created="Wed, 16 Sep 2026 17:33:45 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 14" created="Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:52:54 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="14" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I guess now may be the time to upgrade my Macbook, I had not upgraded it to Tahoe (macOS 26) given all the negativity around that version. " created="Mon, 14 Sep 2026 21:24:23 GMT"/>
				<outline text="The upgrade has finished and I am still amazed by the fact that Apple provides no &quot;out of the box&quot; experience for version upgrades. How should I find out about what is new? I asked Siri and per usual all I got was a list of links to read, I hoped she would be smart enough to show me what is new. " created="Mon, 14 Sep 2026 21:19:31 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Apple has released iPadOS 27 along with iOS 27 and macOS 27 and it is currently downloading to my iPad Mini 6." created="Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:52:54 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 9" created="Wed, 09 Sep 2026 15:11:38 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="09" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Today is another Apple event day and the speculation is that they will be announcing their foldable, possibly named iPhone Duo, which I doubt as that seems to much like the old, failed, Microsoft Surface Duo. As the prices of Apple's products have gone up over the last year I have grown less enthusiastic about their products. I can't image spending nearly $2k on any device." created="Wed, 09 Sep 2026 15:30:17 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Installed V3.15.15 of the Viwoods software on my AI Paper Mini. " created="Wed, 09 Sep 2026 15:12:20 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I think that I am missing posts here, somehow. The only thing that I can think of is that I closed the browser tab before the outline was saved." created="Wed, 09 Sep 2026 15:11:38 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 2" created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:18:27 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="02" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="The reason why I am testing Zen is that I use the &quot;old&quot; Arc browser on my Macbook and I am looking for a similar browser under Linux as well as a possible alternate to Arc should it completely go away. I know that continue use of it is a bit of a security risk." created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 21:53:27 GMT"/>
				<outline text="The default configuration for the VM was 4 GB of RAM and Zen was being shutdown, so I have doubled the RAM to 8 GB. Now system monitor is showing 3.8 GB of RAM being used with Zen loaded." created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 21:03:39 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I am testing the Zen browser running in Fedora. I've created a workspace dedicated to Drummer. Working on some keyboard shortcuts.   " created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:18:27 GMT">
					<outline text="Maximize window Super + Up arrow" created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 20:46:10 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Restore window Super + Down arrow" created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 20:46:22 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Switch to prior workspace Alt + Shift + Left arrow" created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 20:46:36 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Switch to next workspace Alt + Shift + Right arrow" created="Wed, 02 Sep 2026 20:47:03 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="September 1" created="Tue, 01 Sep 2026 16:19:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="It's the first day of September but the temperature and humidity make it feel like the first day of August." created="Tue, 01 Sep 2026 16:19:45 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="August 2026" created="Sun, 02 Aug 2026 16:01:08 GMT" name="august2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="August 30" created="Sun, 30 Aug 2026 17:05:38 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="30" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I've decided that I want to dabble with alternate desktop environments, which I am going to do by running a Fedora 44 VM on my Macbook. The default desktop with this build is Gnome, so that is what I am trying first. So far I have not installed any alternate apps so I am using Firefox 154. " created="Sun, 30 Aug 2026 19:54:42 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Muir league fantasy football draft was last night. I got the first pick and somehow Yahoo gave me an A+ grade, projecting me to finish first and only lose one game. Yeah, not so sure about that but I have a reason to cheer on the Detroit Lions as I have Jahmyr Gibbs. " created="Sun, 30 Aug 2026 17:05:38 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 26" created="Wed, 26 Aug 2026 16:04:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="We have configured an offsite sync of the Proxmox Backup Server backup datastore to BackBlaze B2 and the sync job has run successfully for two nights. Another nightly sync is being done to an attached USB disk. If I were still working in an office I would rotate the USB drives for another offsite backup but I don't so I can't. At this point the USB copy is for convenience more than protection." created="Wed, 26 Aug 2026 16:04:46 GMT"/>
				<outline text="We also got email notifications working via Gmail so that I will now get a heads up if there are problems in the future with any of my Proxmox hosts. Of course, that relies on the Internet and so if the connectivity to the Internet is down the notifications will not get through." created="Wed, 26 Aug 2026 16:07:48 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 23" created="Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:53:37 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="23" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Confirmed that the sync job ran successfully overnight. Set up a verify job for the off-server datastore and confirmed the primary Backups datastore should also have a verify job. Set up both verify jobs and ran first verification manually." created="Sun, 23 Aug 2026 17:17:04 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Troubleshooting of this outage discovered that the backups made by Proxmox Backup Server are within a .RAW file on a separate disk (sda1) from the NVMe disk. Good that it's off the primary disk but bad that it's not more directly accessible. So we add a sync job that copies the primary backup to a datastore on a USB disk. Ideally I will rotate the USB disks and keep one off site but I want to consider using cloud storage for an offsite copy." created="Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:53:37 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 20" created="Thu, 20 Aug 2026 15:57:35 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="20" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="We have successfully restored pvehost2. A baseline report shows that the drive is healthy and at expected values. The only one container appears to have been corrupted, the one running the Pulse monitoring tool. I have restored that container from a backup I made in July and it appears to now be functional." created="Thu, 20 Aug 2026 21:23:48 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Finished the two passes of ddrescue to copy the Crucial P2 to the Western Digital SN850X. Analyzing the rescue map and log." created="Thu, 20 Aug 2026 15:57:35 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 19" created="Wed, 19 Aug 2026 21:26:07 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="19" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Received the replacement 2 TB NVMe SSD that I ordered for pvehost2. Running ddresecue right now to copy the contents of the Crucial P2 to the new drive. With luck I may have pvehost2 back up this evening. " created="Wed, 19 Aug 2026 21:26:07 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 16" created="Sun, 16 Aug 2026 15:59:49 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="16" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Installed &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/FreshRSS/FreshRSS/tree/edge/Docker#docker-compose&quot;&gt;FreshRSS docker container&lt;/a&gt; on docker.home. " created="Sun, 16 Aug 2026 17:13:39 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Yesterday I had the thought that the backups made by Proxmox Backup Server should be on sda in the server so I mounted the disk but did not find the data store. It looks like what I thought was being written to sda is really the virtual disk of the VM, so I will need to fix that after I do the restore. I also need to figure out a better way to get those backup files off that server." created="Sun, 16 Aug 2026 15:59:49 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 14" created="Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:22:43 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="14" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Claude and I did some troubleshooting of the hard drive failure and determined the drive is in a read-only mode, put there by the firmware because there are no more available spare sectors/blocks on the NVMe drive. Basically, the drive wore out much sooner than it should have. " created="Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:22:43 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Claude says of this Crucial P2 drive: &quot;Micron/Crucial silently swapped the P2's NAND from TLC to QLC on some production batches without renaming the SKU. Later-batch P2s have been reported failing with premature spare exhaustion at low reported wear — the wear-leveling counter (Percentage Used) is based on the original NAND's rated program/erase cycles, but the substituted NAND may not survive the same P/E budget. If this drive is a later-batch unit, this failure mode is the known signature.&quot; " created="Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:25:37 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I only paid $63 for this 1 TB NVMe drive, so it clearly was low end and not one I should have used for a Proxmox host. I didn't originally buy it for that purpose, it was used for a Raspberry Pi and re-purposed. Unfortunately, SSD prices are much higher today, a comparable SSD now costs $165 and I now need a 2 TB drive to do the restore and swap and the right SSD will cost me $300 more than that original one." created="Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:27:42 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 13" created="Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:42:55 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="13" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Some time in the last three days the primary hard drive in my main Proxmox host failed. I might have lost some key services, but I have hope that back ups are recoverable off a second drive in the server. I need to wait for my right hand to heal before I can do some serious troubleshooting." created="Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:42:55 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="August 2" created="Sun, 02 Aug 2026 16:01:09 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="02" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Not much happened technologically here during July." created="Sun, 02 Aug 2026 16:01:08 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="July 2026" created="Wed, 01 Jul 2026 14:33:22 GMT" name="july2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="July 27" created="Mon, 27 Jul 2026 15:55:34 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="27" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="We drove 3,384 miles on the Colorado road trip." created="Mon, 27 Jul 2026 15:55:34 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="July 26" created="Sun, 26 Jul 2026 17:11:28 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Returned from our road trip to Colorado. Ting app reported one power disruption during that time that lasted three minutes and our Proxmox servers uptime was not impacted, showing that the UPS I put them on before we left did it's job. " created="Sun, 26 Jul 2026 17:11:28 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="July 7" created="Tue, 07 Jul 2026 15:40:51 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="07" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Bought an APC UPS for my home servers yesterday. I have all three of my servers and the KVM for one connected for battery backup, though there is no connectivity for shutdown. I basically put this in place for handling the occasional brownouts that we get that seem to last for just a few minutes." created="Tue, 07 Jul 2026 15:40:51 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="July 3" created="Fri, 03 Jul 2026 15:50:09 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="It's probably because I am old, but I find &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.weaverobotics.com/isaac-1&quot;&gt;this robot&lt;/a&gt; creepy. I am definitely old." created="Fri, 03 Jul 2026 15:50:09 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="July 1" created="Wed, 01 Jul 2026 14:33:23 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="It's July! " created="Wed, 01 Jul 2026 14:33:22 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="June 2026" created="Mon, 08 Jun 2026 19:03:06 GMT" name="june2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="June 26" created="Fri, 26 Jun 2026 16:00:04 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Learned yesterday that &lt;a href=&quot;https://om.co/2026/06/24/1966-2026/&quot;&gt;Om Malik passed away&lt;/a&gt;. He was just 59 years old but had been living with a heart condition for several years. I did not know him personally but did follow his work and appreciated his writing. He will be missed by many." created="Fri, 26 Jun 2026 16:03:09 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Wear OS 7 has just been pushed to my Pixel Watch. First annoyance I see is that when I swipe tiles right the first I now see is &quot;Add New&quot; rather than the SmartThings tile I had set to control my lights." created="Fri, 26 Jun 2026 16:00:04 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="June 16" created="Tue, 16 Jun 2026 19:12:48 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="16" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://9to5google.com/2026/06/16/google-android-17-pixel-launch/&quot;&gt;Google has released Android 17&lt;/a&gt;, and I just initiated the process to download and install it on my Pixel 10. 9to5Google has a &lt;a href=&quot;https://9to5google.com/2026/06/16/this-is-every-new-feature-in-android-17-video/?__readwiseLocation=&quot;&gt;video overview&lt;/a&gt; of all the changes, which are mostly cosmetic." created="Tue, 16 Jun 2026 19:12:48 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="June 13" created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 15:56:22 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="13" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.versowriter.app/&quot;&gt;Verso&lt;/a&gt; is a word processor for the Mac that costs $14.99 with no subscription." created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 20:21:22 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;b&gt;Google Gemini&lt;/b&gt;" created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 20:22:25 GMT" collapse="true">
					<outline text="I replaced the standard, original, Google One subscription with &lt;a href=&quot;https://support.google.com/googleone/answer/16548195?hl=en&amp;co=GENIE.Platform%3DAndroid&quot;&gt;Google AI Plus&lt;/a&gt;, which provides more AI features for $4.99 per month. I now have access to the Daily Brief produced by Gemini using Gmail, Calendar, and Text Messaging that I find useful but surprised I am not receiving notifications about them each day, instead I have to go in to the Gemini app to read them, which makes no sense. I found where I control whether I see notifications for Daily Brief and toggled that on/off. " created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 15:56:22 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Another problem I have with Gemini on the phone is that it is too verbose. When I ask it for information it produces several paragraphs and seems to insist on reading all of it when I prefer to read what was produced in silence." created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 16:05:55 GMT"/>
					<outline text="One more complaint... I like &lt;a href=&quot;https://notebooklm.google.com&quot;&gt;NotebookLM&lt;/a&gt; but I wish Google would do one of two things, either enable NotebookLM to access Google Keep or provide users the ability to create and edit notebook sources from a phone. Here is my use case. I religiously track the oil level in our 2013 GMC Terrain in a note in Google Keep. I found it useful to provide that odometer and oil level log to NotebookLM so that I can query it, but to get the log into a notebook I have to send a copy to Google Docs because NotebookLM only knows about Google Docs stored in Drive. Keep does not use Drive for storage, and I suspect Google stores notes in a backend database." created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 16:11:23 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Ideally, NotebookLM would automatically sync/update Google Drive sources, but right now I have to manually sync to get updates which is fine except that if I simply send a Keep note to a Google Doc it doesn't overwrite previously shared documents instead it creates another doc with the exact same name and the new &quot;version&quot; of the doc needs to be added as a source. In short, I don't have an easy way to get updates to my oil log in to NotebookLM." created="Sat, 13 Jun 2026 16:22:30 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="June 12" created="Fri, 12 Jun 2026 15:48:00 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="12" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://ews.kylemcdonald.net/&quot;&gt;Apocalypse Early Warning System&lt;/a&gt;: &quot;In the event of an imminent nuclear apocalypse, we suspect that many people who have access to private jets will immediately take to the skies and escape city centers. This site tracks this indicator in realtime. The current emergency level is reported on a scale of 1 to 5, with 5 being an indicator of a likely imminent apocalypse.&quot; Interesting if for nothing more than seeing how many private jets are in the air.  " created="Fri, 12 Jun 2026 15:48:00 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="June 9" created="Tue, 09 Jun 2026 14:40:23 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="09" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I have been avoiding upgrading to MacOS 26 due to all the negativity with Liquid Glass, however, now that MacOS 27 is on the horizon I am thinking that I will need to bite the bullet sooner rather than later." created="Tue, 09 Jun 2026 14:43:05 GMT"/>
				<outline text="At this point I would switch to Viwoods To-dos except that there is no export/archive feature, which I have come to rely upon." created="Tue, 09 Jun 2026 14:40:23 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;b&gt;Apple 2026 WWDC Announcements&lt;/b&gt;" created="Tue, 09 Jun 2026 15:15:45 GMT" collapse="true">
					<outline text="Starting to read others posts regarding Apple's announcements during WWDC. " created="Tue, 09 Jun 2026 15:15:00 GMT"/>
					<outline text="One post says that Shortcuts is gaining the ability to save data, which is something like local variables. " created="Tue, 09 Jun 2026 15:16:19 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="June 8" created="Mon, 08 Jun 2026 19:03:07 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Received notification on Viwoods AIPaper Mini that a new version has been found: V3.15.5. I didn't see any negative feedback on reddit, so I am downloading and installing. I see they added support for handwritten Todos, so I might switch to it from Tasks. Marking support has been added to Papers that provides me the ability to add bookmarks to sections in Papers documents that I created, which I will also use. There is also an outlining feature to explore." created="Mon, 08 Jun 2026 19:03:06 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="May 2026" created="Sun, 03 May 2026 17:04:59 GMT" name="may2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="May 26" created="Tue, 26 May 2026 15:03:41 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="It's Ruth's birthday!" created="Tue, 26 May 2026 15:03:41 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="May 25" created="Mon, 25 May 2026 15:36:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="25" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I am experiencing some very strange keyboard and mouse behavior with my Macbook. It started suddenly last night. I is having bouts of doing whatever it wants to do. I wonder if this is some type of issue with Bluetooth. I briefly had the mouse using the USB dongle, I have switched it back to Bluetooth to see whether the eradic behavior re-appears." created="Mon, 25 May 2026 15:36:46 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="May 6" created="Wed, 06 May 2026 17:35:21 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="06" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.monarchlauncher.com/&quot;&gt;Monarch&lt;/a&gt; looks like an alternate to Raycast. I have Raycast installed on my Macbook but I really only use it as an app launcher, I suspect I can do so much more with it." created="Wed, 06 May 2026 20:24:32 GMT"/>
				<outline text="As attractive as I find Remarkable's tablets, I have a hard time accepting their limitations for the price. A single function device, which can handle EPUB but not Kindle, for $400 seems too much. If Remarkable made a black and white version of the Remarkable Pro Move and sold it for $300 I purchase one right away. IMHO a single use device shouldn't cost much more than $300." created="Wed, 06 May 2026 20:25:41 GMT"/>
				<outline text="The Pure has a 226 PPI, non front lit screen. Not a Wacom stylus.  Seems as though all of the eInk tablet providers are moving away from Wacom and I am not convinced that is a good thing. It means we are locked in to the tablet providers styluses." created="Wed, 06 May 2026 17:41:40 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Remarkable has started selling &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/nJ_plQJP5Zg?si=5iZVXMXiHAyGznVl&quot;&gt;the Pure&lt;/a&gt; and it costs $399 with the marker. My main problem with Remarkable is that it really is only for writing and doesn't provide for installing reading apps like Kindle. It just seems to me that for $400 one should be able to read and write. " created="Wed, 06 May 2026 17:35:21 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="May 3" created="Sun, 03 May 2026 17:05:00 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Welcome to May!" created="Sun, 03 May 2026 17:04:59 GMT" flBulletedSubs="true"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="April 2026" created="Wed, 01 Apr 2026 15:47:50 GMT" name="april2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="April 30" created="Thu, 30 Apr 2026 15:50:36 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="30" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="The &lt;a href=&quot;https://microblog.dev/&quot;&gt;Build With Micro.blog&lt;/a&gt; site documents the micro.blog API. " created="Thu, 30 Apr 2026 15:50:36 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 21" created="Tue, 21 Apr 2026 20:39:43 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="21" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="The notification that version 3.13.8 of the Viwoods software is available appeared on my AI Paper Mini and the upgrade is Downloading. I have not seen much feedback about the update in Reddit. The update file size is 332.75 MB. Installation is taking a while, it is now at 50%. Update completed successfully but I still don't understand why To-Dos and Tasks both exist now that To-Dos has some support for handwriting." created="Tue, 21 Apr 2026 20:39:43 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 19" created="Sun, 19 Apr 2026 17:40:44 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="19" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://lightwhale.asklandd.dk/&quot;&gt;Lightwhale&lt;/a&gt; is a minimal OS image optimized for running Docker containers. I've uploaded the ISO to pvehost1 and built a VM using information provided &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.virtualizationhowto.com/2026/04/i-thought-i-had-the-best-docker-os-in-my-home-lab-until-i-tried-this/?shem=dsdf,sharefoc,agadiscoversdl,,sh/x/discover/m1/4&amp;__readwiseLocation=&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;. I gave the VM two cores and 2 GB of RAM, after boot and making the storage persistent it is only using 450 MB of RAM. I have not pulled any containers. Decided to remove the VM after realizing this is really a bleeding edge distro." created="Sun, 19 Apr 2026 17:40:44 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 18" created="Sat, 18 Apr 2026 15:13:41 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="18" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Happy birthday to me!" created="Sat, 18 Apr 2026 15:13:41 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 11" created="Sat, 11 Apr 2026 16:00:52 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="11" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://shottr.cc/&quot;&gt;Shottr&lt;/a&gt; is a tiny and fast mac screenshot tool with annotations, beautiful backgrounds, scrolling screenshots and S3 upload capabilities. Built with love and optimized for Apple silicon." created="Sat, 11 Apr 2026 16:00:52 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 10" created="Fri, 10 Apr 2026 16:55:09 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="10" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/akashikprotocol/wikimind&quot;&gt;Wikimind&lt;/a&gt; takes a folder of documents and turns them in to a structured wiki." created="Fri, 10 Apr 2026 16:55:09 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 8" created="Wed, 08 Apr 2026 16:13:36 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.flickr.com/photos/nasa2explore/&quot;&gt;NASA's Flicker account&lt;/a&gt; has some awesome photos from the Artemis II mission. Decided to use a few of them as wallpaper on my iPad Mini. NASA has also created &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nasa.gov/artemis-ii-mobile-wallpapers/&quot;&gt;a page on their web site for wallpapers&lt;/a&gt;." created="Wed, 08 Apr 2026 16:13:36 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 3" created="Fri, 03 Apr 2026 14:43:28 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Very cool &lt;a href=&quot;https://artemis-tracker.netlify.app/&quot;&gt;dashboard for the Artemis II mission&lt;/a&gt; around the moon. There is so much more technology now than when we last traveled to the moon 50 years ago, including our ability to check in on the voyage via the Internet." created="Fri, 03 Apr 2026 14:51:59 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Really nice weather today for opening day for the Detroit Tigers at Comerica Park." created="Fri, 03 Apr 2026 14:43:28 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="April 1" created="Wed, 01 Apr 2026 15:47:51 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I am beginning to think the StandbyMode app for Android that I have running on my Pixel 10 is impacting wireless charging.  Playing with it to see if I can determine that for certain." created="Wed, 01 Apr 2026 16:15:38 GMT"/>
				<outline text="It's the first day of April and you know what that means!" created="Wed, 01 Apr 2026 15:47:50 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="March 2026" created="Sun, 01 Mar 2026 16:22:59 GMT" name="march2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="March 27" created="Fri, 27 Mar 2026 15:16:23 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="27" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Manton also recommended upgrading to Hugo 0.158 to speed up publishing time. After I updated no posts appeared on the On This Day page so I reverted back to 0.91. Replied to manton's post regarding this issue." created="Fri, 27 Mar 2026 15:28:51 GMT"/>
				<outline text="A new version of &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/stats/&quot;&gt;the stats&lt;/a&gt; plugin that I use on my blog has been released and I am going to upgrade to it. That was quick and easy and the new stats page looks great!" created="Fri, 27 Mar 2026 15:16:23 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 26" created="Thu, 26 Mar 2026 14:58:40 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="It's opening day! Go, Cubs go!" created="Thu, 26 Mar 2026 14:58:40 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 24" created="Tue, 24 Mar 2026 15:01:05 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="24" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I've seen information on 9to5Google that the Fitbit issue with Pixel Watch was probably a backend issue and is or will shortly be resolved by Google. I've notice that since my actions to correct that the step count has been consistently meeting expectations." created="Tue, 24 Mar 2026 15:01:05 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 18" created="Wed, 18 Mar 2026 14:38:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="18" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Plugins being developed to &lt;a href=&quot;https://adventure.micro.blog/preview/&quot;&gt;embed maps in to micro.blog posts&lt;/a&gt;." created="Wed, 18 Mar 2026 14:38:46 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 17" created="Tue, 17 Mar 2026 15:28:59 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="17" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/parksb/simpesys?tab=readme-ov-file&quot;&gt;Simpesys&lt;/a&gt; is a Markdown file–based documentation build tool that also serves as a complete documentation system for digital gardens" created="Tue, 17 Mar 2026 15:54:24 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Happy St. Patrick's Day!" created="Tue, 17 Mar 2026 15:28:59 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 15" created="Sun, 15 Mar 2026 15:04:28 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="15" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I've been getting notifications on my &lt;a href=&quot;https://support.google.com/googlepixelwatch/answer/16788035?hl=en&quot;&gt;Pixel Watch for the new hand gestures&lt;/a&gt;, today I just got around to checking it out. Gestures need to be made using the hand/wrist on which the watch is worn." created="Sun, 15 Mar 2026 15:34:42 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/Termix-SSH/Termix&quot;&gt;Termix&lt;/a&gt; is an open-source, forever-free, self-hosted all-in-one server management platform. It provides a multi-platform solution for managing your servers and infrastructure through a single, intuitive interface. Termix offers SSH terminal access, remote desktop control (RDP, VNC, Telnet), SSH tunneling capabilities, remote SSH file management, and many other tools. Termix is the perfect free and self-hosted alternative to Termius available for all platforms." created="Sun, 15 Mar 2026 15:04:28 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 14" created="Sat, 14 Mar 2026 17:00:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="14" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I've taken Inkwell out of my tab lineup because it doesn't fit in to my flow. I also find it really slow to render the Hacker News feed. I think I am just too used to the river of news format." created="Sat, 14 Mar 2026 17:00:46 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 13" created="Fri, 13 Mar 2026 20:05:27 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="13" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://wiki.lilygo.cc/get_started/en/Wearable/T-Deck-Plus/T-Deck-Plus.html&quot;&gt;LILYGO T-Deck&lt;/a&gt; is a highly integrated multi-functional embedded development platform based on the ESP32-S3 main control chip, integrating a 2.8-inch 320x240 resolution ST7789 display, trackball navigation module (with directional keys and BOOT button), physical keyboard interface (via I²C communication), TF card storage expansion, LoRa wireless communication module (supporting SCK/MISO/MOSI and control pins), and ES7210 microphone array (for audio input)" created="Fri, 13 Mar 2026 20:05:27 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Link to &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.amazon.com/LILYGO-ESP32-S3-Development-Meshcore-Firmware/dp/B0FYG4D54G&quot;&gt;buy on Amazon&lt;/a&gt;" created="Fri, 13 Mar 2026 20:05:59 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/0015/PocketSSH?__readwiseLocation=&quot;&gt;Pocket SSH Term&lt;/a&gt; turns it in to a portal SSH terminal" created="Fri, 13 Mar 2026 20:06:59 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 12" created="Thu, 12 Mar 2026 15:31:45 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="12" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Day 2 for using the mechanical keyboard. I am surprised by how sensitive it is, I notice this particularly when I am reading web pages and have my finger how the page down key. I don't have to press it all the way down and too often it engages when I do not want it to. I also notice that my typing is not as accurate, probably because I got used to typing on flat keys." created="Thu, 12 Mar 2026 15:31:45 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 11" created="Wed, 11 Mar 2026 15:01:50 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="11" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Received &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0D3F2XX98&quot;&gt;another mechanical keyboard&lt;/a&gt; to try, this one recommended by my friend Todd. It is a low profile version with wireless connectivity at a very affordable price, so this is probably the first one I should have tried. So far I am not feeling the pain that I felt with the prior one and I have not had to adjust my seat. The command and opt keys are much smaller than the Logitech keyboard I normally use. " created="Wed, 11 Mar 2026 15:01:50 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 10" created="Tue, 10 Mar 2026 16:25:01 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="10" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Here is &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/55AISa-jM3o?si=AmyLjEvlE43cXLQU&quot;&gt;a video overview of Inkwell&lt;/a&gt;. I found out one can select text to include in a blog post as a quote." created="Tue, 10 Mar 2026 16:58:21 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Manton has released his RSS feeds reader, &lt;a href=&quot;https://micro.ink/&quot;&gt;Inkwell&lt;/a&gt;. Importing the OPML from Feedland to test. It says that it imported 132 of 135 feeds.  A cool feature is that it integrates with micro.blog such that when I click &quot;New Post&quot; it loads the micro.blog blog post page with a link to the article.  One thing that would be useful is a way to share / quote text from within the item to the blog post. There is also an ability to reply to I am guessing items originating from micro.blog. &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/2026/03/10/manton-reece-has-released-his.html&quot;&gt;This post&lt;/a&gt; originated from Inkwell." created="Tue, 10 Mar 2026 16:25:01 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 8" created="Mon, 09 Mar 2026 03:44:10 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.elecrow.com/crowpanel-esp32-4-2-e-paper-hmi-display-with-400-300-resolution-black-white-color-driven-by-spi-interface.html&quot;&gt;The CrowPanel&lt;/a&gt; is an e-Ink display with a programmable ESP32 microcontroller." created="Mon, 09 Mar 2026 03:44:10 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 7" created="Sat, 07 Mar 2026 16:14:55 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="07" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Another water main break along 14 mile, this time after Drake road. Slight affect on water pressure. Under boil water advisory." created="Sat, 07 Mar 2026 16:14:55 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 4" created="Wed, 04 Mar 2026 15:44:01 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="04" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/radiocolin/Gazette&quot;&gt;Gazette&lt;/a&gt; is a lightweight Gmail-to-RSS bridge designed specifically for reading newsletters in standard RSS readers (like NetNewsWire, Reeder, or FreshRSS). It polls your Gmail for newsletters, strips out the tracking scripts and complex CSS, and serves them as a clean, readable feed." created="Wed, 04 Mar 2026 15:44:01 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 3" created="Tue, 03 Mar 2026 15:36:00 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Finished walking the distance of California, which &lt;a href=&quot;http://frankm.info&quot;&gt;I started tracking in December, 2024&lt;/a&gt;" created="Thu, 05 Mar 2026 16:08:50 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://distrobox.it/&quot;&gt;Distrobox&lt;/a&gt;: Use any Linux distribution inside your terminal. " created="Tue, 03 Mar 2026 15:56:31 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://help.obsidian.md/cli&quot;&gt;Obsidian now has a command line interface&lt;/a&gt; for automations and scripting." created="Tue, 03 Mar 2026 15:36:00 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 2" created="Mon, 02 Mar 2026 16:55:26 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="02" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I am thinking about another Python program to convert all of my monthly Daynotes OPML files to markdown so that they are easier to be read by LLMs. " created="Mon, 02 Mar 2026 16:55:26 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="March 1" created="Sun, 01 Mar 2026 16:23:00 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I want a script that can convert this OPML file to a markdown file. Decided to see whether Claude could help me create a script. I first asked Claude what it knew about OPML and it responded that it is an XML format created by Dave Winer and provided a overview of the structure of the file. I then write the following: " created="Sun, 01 Mar 2026 16:44:18 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&quot;I would like to use an OPML file as a source to generate a markdown file. The OPML file has multiple nodes, some of which have type attributes and I would like created as markdown heads as follows. The text of a node with the type &quot;calendarMonth&quot; should be generated as a markdown H2. The text of a node with the type &quot;calendarDay&quot; should be generated as a markdown H3. All outline nodes contained within a node of type &quot;calendarDay&quot; should be generated as markdown bulleted list item.&quot; " created="Sun, 01 Mar 2026 18:19:00 GMT"/>
				<outline text="In the end I had a Python script that a made a perfect markdown version of this file." created="Mon, 02 Mar 2026 16:08:31 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Dave has written some &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/opmlProjectEditor/&quot;&gt;context/prompt instructions for Claude to interpret OPML&lt;/a&gt; for generation of program source code." created="Sun, 01 Mar 2026 16:40:03 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Welcome to the first day of meteorological spring! " created="Sun, 01 Mar 2026 16:22:59 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="February 2026" created="Sun, 01 Feb 2026 16:07:05 GMT" name="february2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="February 28" created="Sat, 28 Feb 2026 16:05:54 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="28" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="The finance addition to the Pixel At-A-Glance widget is now available on my phone. It displays the top movers of my watch list and I wish it had more customization options. I would prefer to always display specific items to which I have the most shares in my portfolio." created="Sat, 28 Feb 2026 16:23:19 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Decided to move the links to other sites to the top of this outline for faster access." created="Sat, 28 Feb 2026 16:05:54 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 26" created="Thu, 26 Feb 2026 17:10:34 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Google is starting to roll out &lt;a href=&quot;https://9to5google.com/2026/02/25/pixel-at-a-glance-sports-finance/&quot;&gt;the Sports and Finance customizations to the Pixel At A Glance widget&lt;/a&gt;. I have the Sports setting on my Pixel 10 but the Finance setting has not yet appeared." created="Thu, 26 Feb 2026 17:10:34 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 23" created="Mon, 23 Feb 2026 16:54:33 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="23" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Dave posted on micro.blog about posting to micro.blog from Drummer, and I added to &lt;a href=&quot;https://lillihub.com/timeline/84808535#84808535&quot;&gt;the thread&lt;/a&gt; my experience of a lag between the ping and the post that Anton stated he also experiences. It has Manton's attention." created="Mon, 23 Feb 2026 17:10:02 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Yesterday I successfully tested HTTP network access to Feedland after receiving instruction on &lt;a href=&quot;https://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/2026/02/23/164822.html&quot;&gt;config.json changes&lt;/a&gt; to make it work. " created="Mon, 23 Feb 2026 16:54:33 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 21" created="Sat, 21 Feb 2026 19:53:41 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="21" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Spent time today testing Scott's Docker Compose for Feedland. I got Feedland up and running with the ability to signup and signon on my Macbook via localhost as instructed by Scott. My attempts to get this running on a Debian or Ubuntu server does not work. It looks like the app is not able to write to mysql because there is no records added to pendingConfirmations. Posted &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scotthansonde/feedlandDockerCompose/issues/8&quot;&gt;an issue&lt;/a&gt;." created="Sat, 21 Feb 2026 19:53:41 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 20" created="Fri, 20 Feb 2026 17:10:36 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="20" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://linkblog.scotthanson.de/2026/02/20/feedland-docker-compose-for-localhost/&quot;&gt;Scott has released&lt;/a&gt; the updated version of his &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scotthansonde/feedlandDockerCompose&quot;&gt;Docker Compose for Feedland&lt;/a&gt; that supports localhost and it provides a scripted way to produce the magicString for login." created="Fri, 20 Feb 2026 17:19:14 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Here is &lt;a href=&quot;https://lwindolf.github.io/rss-feed-index/blogroll.htm&quot;&gt;an index to blogrolls&lt;/a&gt;. By definition, a blogroll is a OPML file that lists RSS feeds. To test, I click on an entry and copy the URL to the clipboard, switch to Feedland and click Menu, Subscribe, From an OPML File URL. I first tried one of the awesomerss feeds and Feedland generated an error, then I tried another and the subscription worked. It doesn't appear as though the indexing validates the OPML file or the feeds. " created="Fri, 20 Feb 2026 17:10:36 GMT"/>
				<outline text="A flaw in the process of subscribing in Feedland file OPML URL is that there isn't a step to categorize the new feeds." created="Fri, 20 Feb 2026 17:14:36 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 19" created="Thu, 19 Feb 2026 18:40:38 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="19" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I've noticed that my linkblog feed created and provided by Wordland has changed when viewed as a river in that it doesn't show links. It looks ok in &lt;a href=&quot;https://feedland.duckdns.org/?mailbox=https://wordland.social/fjmnotes/246129403/rss.xml&quot;&gt;mailbox view&lt;/a&gt;." created="Thu, 19 Feb 2026 18:40:38 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 18" created="Wed, 18 Feb 2026 15:58:07 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="18" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I think I've known about &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.wired.com/story/pixel-10-can-now-share-files-with-apples-airdrop/&quot;&gt;Android's Quick Share&lt;/a&gt; option on my Pixel 10 that works with Apple's Airdrop but I had not tried it until today. Works as advertised." created="Wed, 18 Feb 2026 17:01:48 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Seems like every day I am reading about another, new, RSS reader. I find the appearance of energy around RSS interesting, but note that many sites don't provide RSS feeds and I think that is mostly due to the desire to make money off one's site." created="Wed, 18 Feb 2026 15:58:07 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 17" created="Tue, 17 Feb 2026 16:41:50 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="17" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://vincode.io/2026/02/16/building-zavala.html&quot;&gt;Zavala 4.0&lt;/a&gt; is not backwards compatible with prior versions, so looks like one needs to somehow manually export outlines from prior versions? I don't see any documentation about an upgrade path, so not sure whether one has to manually export files and re-import or if Version 4 helps in that process." created="Tue, 17 Feb 2026 17:26:15 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Scott Hanson has made &lt;a href=&quot;https://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/2026/02/17.html#a164207&quot;&gt;a localhost version&lt;/a&gt; for his Docker compose file for Feedland. Thinking about the best way for me to test this.   " created="Tue, 17 Feb 2026 16:41:50 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 16" created="Mon, 16 Feb 2026 17:04:06 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="16" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="The &lt;a href=&quot;https://store.huion.com/products/huion-ink-eb1011?irclickid=wCjX:R0xPxyZRJBwYdxX6UhNUkuxtey9w2KyWU0&amp;sharedid=&amp;irpid=3568835&amp;irgwc=1&amp;afsrc=1&quot;&gt;Huion Eink Tablet&lt;/a&gt; looks interesting given it's $329 price that includes the tablet, case, and stylus. I get the impression from watching &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/_IzhYX3hp-c?si=gecxuGd8sSbzSYfK&quot;&gt;this video&lt;/a&gt; that Huion is a known competitor to Wacom, I have not heard of them. Even though the tablet supports EMR, I wonder whether my other styluses will work? I don't see this tablet available for purchase on Amazon." created="Mon, 16 Feb 2026 18:46:05 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Manton added an RSS feed for replies/mentions to micro.blog user posts. I don't think &lt;a href=&quot;https://micro.blog/feeds/frankm/mentions.xml&quot;&gt;this feed&lt;/a&gt; is useful to anyone other than myself. I've added it to the &quot;frank&quot; category in my instance of Feedland. A problem is that because this is a separate feed there is no sequencing of the appearance of a reply and the original post in my river, so it is not obvious what is the context of the reply." created="Mon, 16 Feb 2026 17:13:55 GMT"/>
				<outline text="For some reason, the edit I made to the title of a post on my blog that I wrote yesterday is not taking affect and therefore the only way I can think to correct is to delete the post and then re-publish it today. " created="Mon, 16 Feb 2026 17:04:06 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 15" created="Sun, 15 Feb 2026 16:19:16 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="15" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Air quality alerts today, at present Google is reporting the AQI is 151. " created="Sun, 15 Feb 2026 16:19:16 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 13" created="Fri, 13 Feb 2026 22:53:34 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="13" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I spent the afternoon working on and testing &lt;a href=&quot;https://wordland.shanson.de/2026/02/11/feedland-docker-compose/&quot;&gt;Scott Hanson's dockerized version of Feedland&lt;/a&gt;. I've written notes but I don't have the time now to edit and post them, which I will do this weekend. The net is that like the other time I installed Feedland in my home lab, I could not get browser access via HTTP to work. The web browser just simply refuses to load the site and displays an SSL error message. The good news is that I did get access to work via &lt;a href=&quot;https://notthebe.ee/blog/easy-ssl-in-homelab-dns01/&quot;&gt;my Nginx and duckdns.org configuration&lt;/a&gt; in the same manner as I have done with other apps hosted in my home network." created="Fri, 13 Feb 2026 22:53:34 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 12" created="Thu, 12 Feb 2026 16:02:05 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="12" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://wordland.shanson.de/2026/02/11/feedland-docker-compose/&quot;&gt;Feedland has been dockerized&lt;/a&gt;. I'll probably try this out in the home lab." created="Thu, 12 Feb 2026 16:02:05 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 10" created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:36:13 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="10" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Found out that the default max number of feeds one can subscribe to in Feedland is 250, set by maxNewFeedSubscriptions in config.json. I assume that is to throttle the amount of records in the MySQL database. I increased this number to 500, which I expect to not be a problem because I am the only user. " created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 17:07:58 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Just realized that there is no RSS feed for this outline file being served by PagePark, so a reason to keep using the &quot;Old School&quot; version is to have a RSS feed of my day notes. I just confirmed via Feedland that the HTTPS access to &lt;a href=&quot;https://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/rss.xml&quot;&gt;the RSS feed of that site&lt;/a&gt; works." created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 17:02:15 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I took &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/2026/02/10/today-for-the-first-time.html&quot;&gt;a long 30 minute walk&lt;/a&gt; this morning because it is warm and sunny outside. " created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:41:10 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I got HTTPS working for &lt;a href=&quot;https://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/&quot;&gt;the Old School version of my Daynotes&lt;/a&gt;. For now I am going to keep writing my day notes here and I am not sure how or whether I will continue to use the old site. Details about how I got it working is &lt;a href=&quot;https://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/2026/02/09/205300.html&quot;&gt;here&lt;/a&gt; and I shared &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/drummerCms/issues/4#issuecomment-3873457630&quot;&gt;my results in the Github thread&lt;/a&gt;. " created="Tue, 10 Feb 2026 16:36:13 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 9" created="Mon, 09 Feb 2026 16:39:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="09" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="As I have been blogging more about tablets, particularly e-ink tablets, I added &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/categories/tablets/&quot;&gt;a Tablets category&lt;/a&gt; here and today I spent some time adding that category to related posts I have written during the last year." created="Mon, 09 Feb 2026 17:43:27 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Managed to wget download a copy of the minimal template from github. Used VS Code to do a Find and Replace, it found only one entry in which the change needs to be made. Added to my shared github repo from which Digital Ocean apps pulls to https://shared.frankmcpherson.net. Right now the deployment is failing because Github is running a 500. Need to wait for Github's issues to resolve and then initiate a deploy." created="Mon, 09 Feb 2026 17:03:00 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Appears that &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/02/09.html#a151426&quot;&gt;Dave is now aware of the problem with the Old School blogs&lt;/a&gt;. He says the real issue is that the blogs are now served by https and the template they are based on does not enable that. He provides &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/drummerCms/issues/4&quot;&gt;instruction for how to fix&lt;/a&gt;, which starts by downloading a minimal template, editing it, putting the template html file on a public server, and then adding a header variable to the blog outline. Unfortunately at the moment Github is down so I cannot work on the fix." created="Mon, 09 Feb 2026 16:39:46 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 8" created="Sun, 08 Feb 2026 17:06:51 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Downloading and installing V3.12.12 of the software on my Viwoods AI Paper Mini. Update went smooth and I like the new Quick Refresh mode. " created="Sun, 08 Feb 2026 19:55:32 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I am not sure about how long this page is becoming given that I am writing here nearly every day. Feel like at some point I may want to start collapsing days." created="Sun, 08 Feb 2026 17:09:41 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.manton.org/2026/02/07/really-happy-with-the-new.html&quot;&gt;Manton says&lt;/a&gt; that he has been working on a new editor for micro.blog and will be releasing it on Monday. I am looking forward to trying it out, although since I am now primarily using my Macbook I find that most of my blog posts are being written in Drafts. I think I would prefer to continue writing posts in Drummer but I am probably the only person who wishes to do so and therefore not worth Manton's time to fix." created="Sun, 08 Feb 2026 17:06:51 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 6" created="Fri, 06 Feb 2026 16:37:17 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="06" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/hepplerj/claude-transcribe/blob/main/QUICKSTART_CLAUDE.md&quot;&gt;Claude Transcribe&lt;/a&gt; is a Python app that uses Claude API to perform OCR on batches of PDF files. The developer claims it does a decent job at handwriting recognition. Curious about whether this is something that could be used in my Viwoods to Obsidian work flow." created="Fri, 06 Feb 2026 16:37:17 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 4" created="Wed, 04 Feb 2026 16:51:21 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="04" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://lists.opml.org/#1770245942000&quot;&gt;Dave made a reading list&lt;/a&gt; for the Hacker News &quot;stars&quot; from 2025. I opened my instance of Feedland and clicked Tools, Reading Lists, Subscribe to reading list. I then clicked on the link to view the list as a news page and then added it to the bookmarks so that I can open it easily. I notice that there is a new category called hackernewsstars. Finally, I added this category to the news home page tabs." created="Thu, 05 Feb 2026 01:09:14 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Looks like posting to micro.blog from Drummer is completely broken. I need to stop trying to do this." created="Wed, 04 Feb 2026 17:53:49 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://blogbook.my/&quot;&gt;Blogbook&lt;/a&gt; is an app in development that one can use to make PDF and EPUB files by exporting from WordPress, Micro.blog, or Ghost blog to a single Markdown file. Filter by categories, authors, tags, and dates—then open in Marked 3 for PDF, EPUB, and more. I can create a physical book from an export of my micro.blog to Day One, but that takes all posts in a date range while what I really wanted to do was only publish in book format essays. " created="Wed, 04 Feb 2026 17:01:09 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I am 60% done walking the equivalent of the Pacific Crest Trail, tracked using the &lt;a href=&quot;https://walkthedistanceapp.com/&quot;&gt;Walk The Distance app&lt;/a&gt;." created="Wed, 04 Feb 2026 16:51:21 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 3" created="Tue, 03 Feb 2026 16:46:45 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://zavala.vincode.io/&quot;&gt;Zavala&lt;/a&gt; is an outliner for Mac, iOS, and iPadOS. You can import OPML files but it doesn't read/write them. All outlines are stored in a database. I have not found an obvious way to delete an outline and I find that the fact that I cannot read/write OPML files to be disappointing. Fortunately, I still have a copy of Electric Drummer installed on my Mac." created="Tue, 03 Feb 2026 16:46:45 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 2" created="Mon, 02 Feb 2026 15:04:42 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="02" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Yesterday I played with Docker Sandbox and the Claude Code agent, which was my first hands on experience with a AI coding agent. Today I learned about &lt;a href=&quot;https://buildwithpi.ai/&quot;&gt;another coding agent called Pi&lt;/a&gt;. " created="Mon, 02 Feb 2026 16:58:44 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Created the outline for &lt;a href=&quot;https://info.frankmcpherson.net/2026booksread.opml&quot;&gt;the books that I read in 2026&lt;/a&gt; " created="Mon, 02 Feb 2026 15:04:42 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Decided to keep archiving monthly OPML files to Github" created="Mon, 02 Feb 2026 15:05:21 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="February 1" created="Sun, 01 Feb 2026 16:07:06 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="One thing that I wish pagePark would do is provide users with ways to collapse and expand all in an outline." created="Sun, 01 Feb 2026 16:10:55 GMT"/>
				<outline text="The calendar has turned over to February and it is still friggin' cold out! " created="Sun, 01 Feb 2026 16:07:05 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="January 2026" created="Thu, 01 Jan 2026 19:17:46 GMT" name="january2026" type="calendarMonth" collapse="true">
			<outline text="January 31" created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 16:56:01 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="31" flBulletedSubs="false">
				<outline text="&lt;b&gt;Notes About Hosting This Site&lt;/b&gt;" created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 21:31:33 GMT" flBulletedSubs="true">
					<outline text="Basically, what I need is to be able to store a copy of this outline to another server that can be accessed via a proper URL." created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 21:57:30 GMT"/>
					<outline text="FWIW, I notice that &lt;a href=&quot;https://daveverse.org&quot;&gt;https://daveverse.org&lt;/a&gt;, which is where Dave is publishing to Wordpress has the same template as the OldSchool sites, which means there is at least one other way to get a date drive blog running. " created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 21:29:29 GMT"/>
					<outline text="A concern is that the public URL for this outline is https://drummer.land/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/Daynotes.opml so whatever is the cause of the breakage at http://oldschool.scripting.com can also appear with the server hosting Drummer, although if that happens it might affect more people than myself and in particular Dave. " created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 21:26:14 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I took a quick look at &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/oldSchoolBlog/tree/master&quot;&gt;Dave's oldSchoolBlog app&lt;/a&gt; and quickly decided that there is not much chance of my building my own instance of the app to keep this going the way it used to go. " created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 21:23:31 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Do I want to collapse each day after it passes? I think for now I am going to keep them expanded." created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 17:37:32 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Last thing that I have done is to change the forwarding url &lt;a href=&quot;http://daynotes.frankm.info&quot;&gt;http://daynotes.frankm.info&lt;/a&gt; to forward to this page rather than the OldSchool blog page. Looks like I have to wait for DNS to update." created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 17:33:44 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Going forward I will use this one outline for my daily notes. I am going to keep each month in the outline but I am going to collapse the month header after the month has past. I think this will provide an easy archive of my day notes. I need to decide whether I am going to continue creating the monthly OPML files that I have been storing in Github." created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 17:22:56 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I have added an icon to the icon bar in Drummer to replace the plus icon that calls op.insertInCalendar (), which when invoked either creates the calendar headers if they don't exist or creates a new blank node without setting a type attribute. The result is that pagePark renders this outline as I desire." created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 17:02:18 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I've been thinking about what to do if Dave doesn't fix the hosting of the Old School blogs. My decision is to create this second outline of which I think pagePark will do a better job of rendering." created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 16:42:05 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I did not realize this, but it seems that for some reason pagePark has a problem rendering nodes that have a type value. For whatever reason some time it renders the entire node as a link. " created="Sat, 31 Jan 2026 16:47:01 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 30" created="Fri, 30 Jan 2026 16:33:00 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="30" flBulletedSubs="true" collapse="false">
				<outline text="I have created a redirect to this public OPML file to provide an alternate way to get to it than Dave's OldSchool server. The url is &lt;a href=&quot;https://info.frankmcpherson.net/daynotes.opml&quot;&gt;https://info.frankmcpherson.net/daynotes.opml&lt;/a&gt;. There is something really weird going on with serving this file that is causing an entire node to be a URL like this one." created="Fri, 30 Jan 2026 17:00:30 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Getting an error when building &lt;a href=&quot;http://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/&quot;&gt;my blog&lt;/a&gt;, the file name contains illegal characters. I have been curious about how this url: http://oldschool.scripting.com/frank.mcpherson@gmail.com/ has been working given the 'at' sign because Dave chose to use email addresses for the home site.  " created="Fri, 30 Jan 2026 16:34:56 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/andrew-waters/orchard&quot;&gt;Orchard&lt;/a&gt; is a native (swift) application to manage containers on macOS using the new Containerization framework." created="Fri, 30 Jan 2026 16:33:01 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 27" created="Tue, 27 Jan 2026 16:23:32 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="27" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="To me the answer to &lt;a href=&quot;https://micro.blog/ReaderJohn/82907830&quot;&gt;the question in this thread&lt;/a&gt; is so obvious, because I think the natural outcome of religion is idolatry. When you consider that a group of people worship and idol, whether that be a religion, a bible, or even a name then it is easy to understand how those people don't know what is real so much so they don't even care because the idol reinforces their beliefs." created="Tue, 27 Jan 2026 16:23:33 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 20" created="Tue, 20 Jan 2026 16:37:48 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="20" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/rcarmo/agentbox&quot;&gt;Agentbox&lt;/a&gt; is a simple Docker-based coding agent sandbox, originally inspired by running Batrachian Toad as a general-purpose coding assistant TUI and now generalized to more tools. " created="Tue, 20 Jan 2026 16:54:30 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Rui Carmo has published &lt;a href=&quot;https://taoofmac.com/space/blog/2026/01/17/2130?utm_content=atom&quot;&gt;an overview&lt;/a&gt; of his RSS summarization and reading pipeline." created="Tue, 20 Jan 2026 16:37:49 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 13" created="Tue, 13 Jan 2026 16:26:23 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="13" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="Leo Laporte has vibe coded &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/leolaporte/rss-reader&quot;&gt;a terminal RSS reader app&lt;/a&gt; that uses Claude API for summarization. This might provide me with a way to test AI summarization of RSS feeds. And here is &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/travisred/rss-local-storage&quot;&gt;another RSS reader that uses local storage&lt;/a&gt;. It does feel like RSS is making a come back given the number of people making apps for it." created="Tue, 13 Jan 2026 16:33:30 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I am thinking of paying for a monthly subscription to Claude in order to learn more about AI and what it can do for me. " created="Tue, 13 Jan 2026 16:27:47 GMT"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://privatellm.app/en&quot;&gt;PrivateLLM&lt;/a&gt; is a iOS/iPadOS/MacOS app that runs open source LLMs on the device and costs $4.99. The LLMs are not from Hugging Face and optimized for the app." created="Tue, 13 Jan 2026 16:26:24 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 12" created="Mon, 12 Jan 2026 17:29:02 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="12" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I haven't upgraded to Tahoe yet, and &lt;a href=&quot;https://noheger.at/blog/2026/01/11/the-struggle-of-resizing-windows-on-macos-tahoe/&quot;&gt;this is a reason why&lt;/a&gt;. " created="Mon, 12 Jan 2026 17:29:03 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 9" created="Fri, 09 Jan 2026 16:22:54 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="09" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.touch-portal.com/&quot;&gt;Touch Portal&lt;/a&gt; enables one to turn an Android or iOS device in to a stream deck. " created="Fri, 09 Jan 2026 19:37:58 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Found an app, &lt;a href=&quot;https://zetabitapps.com/standbymodepro&quot;&gt;Standby Pro&lt;/a&gt;, that turns my Pixel 10 in to a smart display when I have it on the wireless charger. What I like about it is, I can configure the manner in which the app auto launches. Right now it is configured to only launch when I put the Pixel 10 on the charger in landscape and its connected to Wifi. If I put the phone on the charger in portrait the app does not launch and in my case the phone stays on the lock screen. The app comes with a number of preset configurations but also can display standard Android app widgets, which means that I can have the Windy radar display to show the current weather. I tried Radarscope but that widget doesn't want to work in landscape." created="Fri, 09 Jan 2026 16:22:55 GMT"/>
				<outline text="I have &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.belkin.com/p/2-in-1-foldable-magnetic-charger-with-qi2-25w/BBB010-BL.html&quot;&gt;this Belkin Qi 2.5 wireless charger&lt;/a&gt; that I am really happy with, it charges my phone and ear buds wirelessly at the same time and has a USB-C port to plug in my watch charger. Funny that it wasn't until I tried Standby Pro that I even considered putting the phone on the charger in landscape." created="Fri, 09 Jan 2026 16:38:03 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 8" created="Thu, 08 Jan 2026 21:57:05 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.tropy.org/&quot;&gt;Tropy&lt;/a&gt; is free open-source software that allows you to organize and describe photographs of research material." created="Thu, 08 Jan 2026 21:57:06 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 6" created="Tue, 06 Jan 2026 17:09:49 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="06" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I have not yet upgraded my Macbook Pro to Tahoe. I guess the main reason why is that I haven't seen a compelling reason why I should run toward it. And yet, I do want to keep current so sooner or later I will have to go there." created="Tue, 06 Jan 2026 17:09:50 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 3" created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:26:27 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03">
				<outline text="&lt;b&gt;The Clicks Communicator&lt;/b&gt;" created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:44:03 GMT">
					<outline text="I noticed some entries in my RSS feeds this morning making reference to the Blackberry, and I suspect they were in reference to the &lt;a href=&quot;https://clicksphone.com/communicator&quot;&gt;Clicks Communicator&lt;/a&gt;, a new Android-based phone to launch later this year. This is from &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.clicks.tech/&quot;&gt;the company&lt;/a&gt; that has been selling a physical keyboard case for the iPhone. The Clicks Communicator has a built-in physical keyboard, hence the reference to Blackberry. " created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:26:28 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The device software is built on Android 16 with a customized launcher that is optimized for communications. What it looks to me like is the Android notification shade made front and center to the device. It has hardware buttons to initiate voice to text input, if one prefers and has an LED around the primary button that will flash different colors one configures for different notifications. The color LED reminds me of the roller ball of the first Android phone, the T-Mobile G1. " created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:30:57 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The negatives start with the price, while one can reserve one now for a $399 total price by paying $199 to reserve it, the Communicator will retail for $499, which I think is too much for a device being promoted as a second phone. The intro video compares the Communicator to a Kindle as it compares to the iPad, but Kindles cost much less than $499. " created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:33:41 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Another big miss in my opinion is not providing a removable battery nor Magsafe support, although I imagine this might be something that could be provided via special &quot;cover.&quot; The device includes the things smartphones lost over the years like the physical buttons, storage card slots, and 3.5mm audio adapter, so why not go all the way and provide removable batteries? " created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:35:53 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The Clicks Communicator is going to be a niche device, it's not going to take over the smartphone leaders. I got to think that $499 starting price will have to come down to the $300 to $400 range to justify the purchase as an &quot;accessory&quot; to an every day carry smartphone. The question then is, will enough sell for the device to last long enough for the price to come down? The answer depends on whether there is truly a demand for this type of device." created="Sat, 03 Jan 2026 17:38:32 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 2" created="Fri, 02 Jan 2026 18:24:14 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="02" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="For the first time I used the &lt;a href=&quot;https://dayone.app&quot;&gt;Day One app&lt;/a&gt; to create a book of my blog posts I wrote during the past year. I've known about this ability and have wanted to do it for a while, but it wasn't until recently that I found a way to send all of my posts to the app. I was surprised to find that one can only create and purchase the book on the iOS/iPadOS versions of the app, I wonder why they don't include this feature in the MacOS app. I have also created a PDF of all my blog posts, but the book I ordered is only for the past year." created="Fri, 02 Jan 2026 18:24:15 GMT"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="January 1" created="Thu, 01 Jan 2026 19:17:47 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01" flBulletedSubs="true">
				<outline text="I published my &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/2026/01/01/year-in-books-for.html&quot;&gt;2025 Year In Books&lt;/a&gt; to my blog using &lt;a href=&quot;https://epilogue.micro.blog/&quot;&gt;Epilogue&lt;/a&gt; and wrote &lt;a href=&quot;https://frankmcpherson.blog/2026/01/01/at-the-start-of.html&quot;&gt;my first blog post for the year&lt;/a&gt; in Obsidian. My first attempts to use the micro.publish plugin to publish the post failed and it took me a little bit of time to update the plugin, which resolved the problem" created="Thu, 01 Jan 2026 19:18:04 GMT"/>
				<outline text="Happy New Year! " created="Thu, 01 Jan 2026 19:17:48 GMT" inlineImage="https://frankmcpherson.blog/uploads/2026/b0a932d1d0.jpg"/>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</body>
	</opml>
